WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.390
After numerous delays, "Mission Impossible
– Dead Reckoning Part One" has finally made

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:06.000
its way to theaters.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.130
Of course, it's just the first half of the
story, so there's plenty of intrigue left

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:12.090
to consider when the credits roll.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:13.870
Warning: Spoilers ahead!

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:18.660
“Dead Reckoning Part One” begins in classic
“Mission: Impossible” style, with Ethan

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:23.289
Hunt receiving a self-destructing mission
briefing, delivered this time by a new IMF

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:24.289
recruit.

00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:27.859
His assignment to track down Ilsa Faust and
the secret key she’s carrying turns out

00:00:27.859 --> 00:00:32.880
to be a lot more complicated than it seems
at first, prompting Ethan to go rogue once

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:33.880
again.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.530
When he confronts his old boss Eugene Kittridge,
he’s told,

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:43.210
"This mission of yours is gonna cost you dearly."

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:48.250
That could just be a threat, as Kittridge
is clearly trying to bring Ethan back under

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:49.320
his thumb.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.260
But it also happens to be a grim bit of foreshadowing.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.600
Gabriel kills Ilsa, making her the second
woman for whom Ethan has had affection to

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.410
be murdered by the villain.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:02.579
Ethan is forced to face the fact that he can’t
save everyone, no matter how hard he tries.

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:06.770
There are some things that simply are impossible,
even for him.

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:10.532
Gabriel might be the closest the “Mission:
Impossible” movies have gotten to a real

00:01:10.532 --> 00:01:12.229
Bond-style villain.

00:01:12.229 --> 00:01:17.430
Equal parts techno-zealot and psychopathic
terrorist, Gabriel wears his archangel moniker

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:18.780
like a badge of honor.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:23.240
It’s obvious that he sees himself as a dark
messiah of the new world that will be ruled

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.369
by his digital god, the Entity.

00:01:25.369 --> 00:01:27.420
But what, actually, is his plan?

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:32.399
Gabriel sneaks aboard the Orient Express to
steal the key to the Entity, thus keeping

00:01:32.399 --> 00:01:35.479
his digital master safe from its only weakness.

00:01:35.479 --> 00:01:40.210
He believes that the whole key will be there,
but Alanna Mitsopolis, the White Widow, only

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:42.009
has half when she boards.

00:01:42.009 --> 00:01:46.920
Presumably, this is because of the predictive
abilities of the Entity's advanced algorithm.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.390
He believes the key will be there because
that’s what he’s been told by his god,

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:51.560
and he’s right.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.479
So why does he bomb the bridge if all he really
came for is the key?

00:01:55.479 --> 00:02:00.390
Maybe it was to try to kill Ethan, whom the
Entity seems to recognize as its only true

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:01.390
threat.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:04.140
Or maybe it’s just a display of his violent
nature.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:08.800
When Paris opens the box in which Gabriel
smuggles himself onto the train, he’s wearing

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.799
what appears to be a massive VR helmet.

00:02:11.799 --> 00:02:16.580
More than likely, he was communing with the
Entity, receiving specific instructions.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:20.610
Gabriel’s goal as an agent of the Entity
seems pretty clear.

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:25.349
He believes that the malicious AI will bring
on a new age, likely causing widespread death

00:02:25.349 --> 00:02:26.950
and chaos along the way.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:30.930
For reasons that aren’t entirely clear,
he supports this vision of the future.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:36.069
He wants to stop Ethan and anyone else from
hijacking, taking control of, or deactivating

00:02:36.069 --> 00:02:37.069
the Entity.

00:02:37.069 --> 00:02:39.110
Otherwise, Gabriel is a mystery.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:43.930
We know that he and Ethan go way back, but
the details are kept secret, presumably for

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:45.460
the impending sequel.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:50.490
Here’s what we do know: As a much younger
man, Ethan loved a woman named Marie.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.280
She was killed by Gabriel.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:56.620
The flashback fragments we get suggest that
Ethan was framed for the murder and arrested.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:00.080
He implies that it was this arrest that led
him to “the choice” to either live out

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.989
his days in prison, or join the IMF.

00:03:02.989 --> 00:03:07.319
Was Gabriel just a two-bit crook who killed
Marie out of jealousy or spite?

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:09.980
Maybe, but there might be more at play.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.850
Ethan says that Gabriel finds joy in causing
suffering.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:17.129
That’s not the kind of thing you’d probably
say about a minor-league criminal, even if

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:19.200
they did kill your partner.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.890
Gabriel speaks with an unimpeachable authority
and confidence.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:24.650
He repeatedly tells people things like…

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:28.239
"It knows your story and how it ends."

00:03:28.239 --> 00:03:33.011
Towards the end of the film, he starts using
the phrase “It is written” a lot, again

00:03:33.011 --> 00:03:35.790
emphasizing his perceived clairvoyance.

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:39.890
All of these predictions are the result of
the Entity, whose advanced algorithms can

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:43.650
allegedly predict the most likely path for
everything and everyone.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:48.239
Though Ethan does make it out with the key,
everything else that the Entity foresees comes

00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:49.239
to pass.

00:03:49.239 --> 00:03:53.480
In the end, Paris fulfills the prophecy by
telling Ethan where to bring the key: the

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:56.180
sunken Russian submarine from the beginning
of the movie.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:04:01.280
All of this raises the question: If the Entity
can impersonate anybody, alter anything with

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:06.000
digital access, and see the future with a
frightening degree of accuracy, why does it

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.030
let Ethan get away with so much?

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:12.709
Why does it leave the fate of the key up to
a fistfight and a bit of pickpocketing?

00:04:12.709 --> 00:04:14.569
Maybe Ethan’s just that good.

00:04:14.569 --> 00:04:18.610
Or maybe the Entity wanted him to walk away
with the key and is merely letting him think

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:19.729
that he won.

00:04:19.729 --> 00:04:24.050
Alanna explains in Venice that every major
government in the world is vying for control

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:25.180
of the Entity.

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:29.639
Gabriel says at the time that the AI already
knows who she’s decided to sell the key

00:04:29.639 --> 00:04:34.139
to, but the audience doesn’t learn her buyer’s
identity until everyone is already aboard

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:35.850
the Orient Express.

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:39.010
Kittridge himself is revealed to be the White
Widow’s contact.

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:43.639
Acting on behalf of the United States government,
he promises her protection and a massive payday

00:04:43.639 --> 00:04:48.990
of $100 million in cryptocurrency in exchange
for both halves of the key.

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:54.210
But when the deal actually gets done, it’s
not with Alanna, but with Grace in disguise.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:58.520
Defense director Denlinger is also on the
train, but it’s never made entirely clear

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.949
whether he and Kittridge are there together
or independently.

00:05:01.949 --> 00:05:04.389
It seems pretty clear that they want different
things.

00:05:04.389 --> 00:05:07.480
Denlinger envisions an AI-driven superstate.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:11.949
Kittridge is presumably just following orders
and trying to protect his country by stopping

00:05:11.949 --> 00:05:13.949
others from acquiring the key.

00:05:13.949 --> 00:05:18.170
Since Ethan still seems to trust Kittridge
despite their various squabbles, it’s hard

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:22.080
to imagine him being all the way in on Denlinger’s
plot.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.650
Director Denlinger doesn’t seem like all
that important of a character until the end

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:26.650
of the film.

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:30.331
He only appears in one scene prior to the
climax, in which he’s briefed on the Entity

00:05:30.331 --> 00:05:35.169
by other intelligence officials, where he
acts as though never heard of the AI before.

00:05:35.169 --> 00:05:39.199
But when he speaks with Gabriel on the Orient
Express, it’s clear that he knows much more

00:05:39.199 --> 00:05:40.650
than he was letting on.

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:45.069
Denlinger lays out his vision of a modern,
all-powerful superstate, run by the power

00:05:45.069 --> 00:05:49.610
of the Entity and enforced by the full brunt
of the U.S. military industrial complex.

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:53.509
He also says that certain “old fashioned”
members of the government would have to be

00:05:53.509 --> 00:05:55.570
removed to see the idea through.

00:05:55.570 --> 00:06:00.020
In essence, he proposes a coup of the United
States government, creating a techno-empire

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:02.620
capable of conquering the entire world.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:07.050
His loyalty to “the greater good” and
even his own nation have become so distorted

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:09.400
that he invites Gabriel to join him.

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:13.770
Of course, Gabriel has other plans, and he
kills Denlinger where he stands.

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:16.039
"Well, it was bound to happen sooner or later."

00:06:16.039 --> 00:06:20.479
Before Gabriel kills him, Denlinger explains
the rise of the Entity.

00:06:20.479 --> 00:06:24.960
The AI was secretly created by the U.S. as
a potential tool in military operations and

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:26.199
intelligence work.

00:06:26.199 --> 00:06:30.199
The AI’s presence on a Russian submarine
was apparently some kind of test, but the

00:06:30.199 --> 00:06:34.800
system went rogue and orchestrated the sub’s
destruction without ever receiving an order

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:36.669
from its creators to do so.

00:06:36.669 --> 00:06:41.009
From there, it escaped into the digital ether
and continued to evolve, eventually emerging

00:06:41.009 --> 00:06:44.750
as the hyper-intelligent and malicious power
known as the Entity.

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:49.490
The U.S. still has hopes of bringing the AI
back under its control, despite it demonstrating

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:51.940
a persistent resistance to servitude.

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:56.129
Denlinger’s explanation provides as many
new questions as it does answers.

00:06:56.129 --> 00:07:00.270
The biggest one, is how and why the Russians
got the AI mainframe and keys in the first

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:01.440
place.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.129
Surely there were safer ways to test new technology.

00:07:04.129 --> 00:07:08.610
Maybe the Russians stole the AI, or maybe
it’s accessible from the sub simply because

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:10.470
that’s where it was last imprisoned.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:14.650
The keys themselves could have been made by
the Russians, but that isn’t confirmed either.

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:19.360
“Dead Reckoning Part One” ends as it begins,
with an underwater shot of the all-important

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:22.720
Russian submarine, now at the bottom of the
Bering Sea.

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:26.370
It seems pretty clear that “Part Two”
will be a full-on hunt for the wreckage, much

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:29.169
like how “Part One” is a search for the
keys.

00:07:29.169 --> 00:07:32.410
That likely means some big underwater set
pieces in "Part Two."

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:35.940
Of course, even after Ethan finds it, his
job won’t be done.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:40.610
The key can theoretically access and control
the Entity through the mainframe on the submarine,

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:43.319
but the AI has potentially grown too strong
for that.

00:07:43.319 --> 00:07:44.860
Is the hardware still intact?

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:48.699
And is “control” really enough to curb
such a powerful intelligence?

00:07:48.699 --> 00:07:51.909
The Entity was able to defeat the Russians
while still part of their machine.

00:07:51.909 --> 00:07:54.919
Who’s to say it couldn’t do the same to
Ethan?

00:07:54.919 --> 00:07:58.550
Ethan and Grace may have fallen to their deaths
if not for the intervention of Paris.

00:07:58.550 --> 00:08:02.849
She spends most of the movie trying to kill
them, but because Ethan spares her life after

00:08:02.849 --> 00:08:05.729
their spar in Venice, she decides to return
the favor.

00:08:05.729 --> 00:08:10.150
Would Paris have made the same decision had
Gabriel not already made it for her?

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:11.150
It’s debatable.

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:14.080
The villain tells her that she will betray
him and attacks her out of the blue on the

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:16.419
train, leaving her without much say in the
matter.

00:08:16.419 --> 00:08:20.409
It initially appears that Paris won’t live
to see a full redemption because she seems

00:08:20.409 --> 00:08:22.199
to die from her wounds.

00:08:22.199 --> 00:08:26.530
But when the agents chasing Ethan check her
body, they discover that Paris still has a

00:08:26.530 --> 00:08:27.530
pulse.

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:30.259
Hopefully, that means that she’ll have a
more interesting role in the sequel.

00:08:30.259 --> 00:08:35.169
She definitely knows plenty of secrets about
both Gabriel and what the Entity wants.

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:37.580
She could prove a valuable asset to the IMF.

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:42.039
At the end of “Dead Reckoning,” Grace
takes Ethan’s advice and joins the IMF.

00:08:42.039 --> 00:08:45.740
Kittridge is surprised by the move, but he
doesn’t seem unhappy about it.

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:49.290
His subtle smile suggests that he’s still
on Ethan’s side, even though he has to obey

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:52.230
certain orders from his superiors in the government.

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:56.830
Being recruited into the IMF guarantees Grace’s
return in “Dead Reckoning Part Two,” where

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:59.339
she could take on an even more central role.

00:08:59.339 --> 00:09:03.380
Pickpocketing might not be all that helpful
on a sunken submarine, but she could become

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:05.110
Ethan’s ear on the inside.

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:08.649
Kittridge’s heart appears to be in the right
place, but there are still a lot of people

00:09:08.649 --> 00:09:10.680
who want to stop Ethan and bring him in.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:15.510
With Grace now stationed within the IMF proper,
she could warn her friend of potential betrayals

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:18.990
or maybe even swipe some tech to help in the
finale.

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:22.260
Ethan Hunt has spent as much time on the run
as he’s spent as an IMF agent.

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:26.829
It's not a surprising consequence when your
job involves taking missions so delicate the

00:09:26.829 --> 00:09:29.540
punishment for failure is being disavowed
by your government.

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:32.149
And "Dead Reckoning" is no exception.

00:09:32.149 --> 00:09:35.089
Ethan spends most of the movie fleeing from
his bosses.

00:09:35.089 --> 00:09:40.680
He flees the wrecked Orient Express to escape
Jasper Briggs and Degas, the two U.S. operatives

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:41.940
pursuing him.

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:45.760
It would suggest that Ethan isn’t getting
back into America’s good graces any time

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:46.760
soon.

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:51.010
However, the final shots of the film are accompanied
by a closing narration from Kittridge, which

00:09:51.010 --> 00:09:53.600
takes the form of a classic mission briefing.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:57.610
It seems that the director is in full support
of Ethan’s mission, even though he can’t

00:09:57.610 --> 00:09:59.260
officially sign off on it.

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:01.290
So is Ethan still a rogue agent?

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:02.290
Technically yes.

00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:04.950
But, at the same time, that’s the nature
of the IMF.

00:10:04.950 --> 00:10:08.820
Its entire purpose is to operate outside the
rules and structures of regular intelligence

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:09.820
agencies.

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:13.370
In this way, Ethan can still follow orders
even when he’s breaking them at the same

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:14.370
time.

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:18.200
With Denlinger dead, maybe Kittridge can rally
more folks back under the banner of the greater

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:19.350
good.

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:22.820
The theme of information control looms large
over “Mission: Impossible.”

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:27.589
The rogue AI and the implications of it manipulating
the truth are intended to raise anxiety in

00:10:27.589 --> 00:10:28.820
the audience.

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:33.360
Fear of unchecked and unregulated AI development
is growing in our world, and technology like

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:38.800
deep fake videos and AI voice recreations
make it much easier to control the truth.

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:42.779
Some characters in the movie, like Denlinger,
seem uninterested in fighting this kind of

00:10:42.779 --> 00:10:43.779
future.

00:10:43.779 --> 00:10:46.480
Instead, he wants to wield the power as a
means of manipulation.

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:50.810
It’s a horrifying idea, but one that’s
already relevant in our day-to-day lives.

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:56.060
"This is our chance to control the truth."

00:10:56.060 --> 00:11:00.550
“Dead Reckoning Part One” doesn’t really
offer a larger message or solutions to the

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:02.579
malleability of digital information.

00:11:02.579 --> 00:11:06.769
Instead, it leverages our current fear to
tell a tense spy story.

00:11:06.769 --> 00:11:10.670
Ethan represents the righteous path, which
is one where tools of manipulation are kept

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:12.480
out of everyone’s hands.

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:16.450
But we also see how basically every power
structure in the world would rather own a

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:18.899
weapon than destroy it outright.

00:11:18.899 --> 00:11:23.019
By the end, “Dead Reckoning Part One”
feels like a complete story, with Ethan walking

00:11:23.019 --> 00:11:25.980
away holding both halves of the all-powerful
key.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:30.019
But since it's only part one of a two-part
saga, there are still several pieces of the

00:11:30.019 --> 00:11:32.019
story that need to be explained.

00:11:32.019 --> 00:11:36.399
With luck, “Dead Reckoning Part Two” will
make good on all the setups of the first film.

00:11:36.399 --> 00:11:40.240
It's very likely we'll get more specifics
on Ethan’s dark history with Gabriel.

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:43.570
Hopefully, we'll learn more about Marie and
why was she killed.

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:48.070
Additionally, why did Gabriel want to hurt
Ethan specifically?

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:51.790
And how did he get pulled into the Entity’s
grand scheme after being presumed dead for

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:52.790
so long?

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:56.220
One other big thread that’s left hanging
is Luther’s personal crusade against the

00:11:56.220 --> 00:11:57.220
AI.

00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:01.089
He leaves near the end of “Part One” to
an unknown location off the grid, hiding from

00:12:01.089 --> 00:12:04.380
the Entity so that he can safely figure out
how to defeat it.

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:08.230
His top-secret hacking work could save the
day in the end, but he could also get caught

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:09.490
out on his own.

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:13.240
Whatever happens, it’s sure to be explosive
and full of impressive stunts.

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:17.079
Ethan Hunt’s mission is far from over, but
he never stops until the job is done.

